[s0l0!-header][h1]Accessing Two-Dimensional Arrays[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

To access an element of a two-dimensional array, both the row index and column index are required.
For example, the following statements display the value of an element and then assign a new value: [code format="c" codeId="1671"]int a[2][3] = {
  {3, 2, 6},
  {4, 5, 20}
};
printf("Element 3 in row 2 is %d\n", a[1][2]); /* 20 */
a[1][2] = 25;
printf("Element 3 in row 2 is %d\n", a[1][2]); /* 25 */ [/code]
Just as a [b]for [/b]loop is used to iterate through a one-dimensional array, nested [b]for [/b]loops are used to traverse a two-dimensional array: [code format="c" codeId="1672"]int a[2][3] = {
  {3, 2, 6},
  {4, 5, 20}
};
int k, j;
/* display array contents */
[b]for [/b](k = 0; k < 2; k++) {
  [b]for [/b](j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
    printf(" %d", a[k][j]);
  }
  printf("\n");
}[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

